# The ledger of `font`: what Go's `golang.org/x/image/font`, `font/sfnt`,
# `font/opentype` and `font/basicfont` (x/image v0.48.0; `go doc -all` run
# 2026-11-23 inside the scratch folder), Rust's `ttf-parser` (0.25.1, the
# list of its docs.rs `all.html`, read 2026-11-23: Rust's standard library
# has no font) and Python's `fontTools` (4.68.0, `dir()` of `fontTools.ttLib`,
# `TTFont`, `TTCollection`, `fontTools.subset` and the module names of
# `fontTools.pens`, run 2026-11-23) let a program do with a font, one block per
# capability, and what Rebel answers. Rust's modules and Python's tables are
# named by the module, never one item at a time: `ttf_parser::gpos` is every
# item of that module.
#
# What the tests expect is what fontTools 4.68.0 read from the same files:
# the two samples of `font/internal/samples.hero` (Noto Sans Regular 2.015 cut
# to 29 glyphs, under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, Copyright 2022 The Noto
# Project Authors; the licence text stands in each font's `name` table, id 13
# and 14, and in full at https://github.com/notofonts/latin-greek-cyrillic/blob/main/OFL.txt),
# and, run once outside the tree on 2026-11-23, the whole of ten fonts of this
# Mac (Noto Sans Regular 3,884 glyphs; Arial Unicode MS 50,377; SF NS 2,954;
# Monaco 1,678; Apple SD Gothic Neo, CFF with 18,662 CID-keyed glyphs;
# Hiragino Sans GB; Hiragino Mincho ProN; Hiragino Kaku Gothic W8; Kohinoor;
# LastResort, CFF), their glyph counts, scale, ascent, descent and line gap,
# every character `cmap` maps, the names of ids 1, 2, 4, 6 and 16, and for
# every glyph sampled (the first 24, then one in 1 to 41) the advance, the
# left bearing, the box and the number and coordinate sums of the outline's
# steps: 0 differences in all ten, in Arial Unicode MS's 33 glyphs that hold a
# contour of one point compared with that contour left out, which fontTools
# draws as a `move` and a `close` and this package does not, a point that
# draws nothing.

capability font/parse  a font read from the bytes of a file, a single font or one of a collection
  heroes  font.parse, font.font_count, font.Font
  go      sfnt.Parse, sfnt.ParseCollection, sfnt.Collection, Collection.Font, Collection.NumFonts, sfnt.Font, opentype.Parse, opentype.ParseCollection, opentype.Collection, opentype.Font
  rust    Face::parse, Face::from_slice, fonts_in_collection, RawFace::parse, RawFace::from_slice, RawFace, TableRecord, Tag, FaceParsingError
  python  TTFont, TTCollection, SFNTReader, TTLibError, TTLibFileIsCollectionError
  note    a table whose place runs past the file is `invalid`, where Go reads it lazily and fails at the first use; a TrueType font and an OpenType font with CFF outlines read the same way; a CFF2 font and a Type 1 font are `unsupported`

capability font/parse-reader  a font read through a reader at an offset, so that a large file is never held whole
  heroes  refused: a font is read from the bytes the program holds, which `fs.read_bytes` gives; a reader would hand a font's tables to the program one read at a time with no gain, the largest font files being tens of megabytes
  go      sfnt.ParseReaderAt, sfnt.ParseCollectionReaderAt, opentype.ParseReaderAt, opentype.ParseCollectionReaderAt, Font.WriteSourceTo
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

capability font/names  the family, style, PostScript name, copyright, licence and other strings of a font
  heroes  font.name_of, font.NameId
  go      Font.Name, sfnt.NameID and its constants (NameIDCopyright to NameIDVariationsPostScriptPrefix)
  rust    name::Table, name::Names, name::Name, Face::names
  python  TTFont['name'], table__n_a_m_e.getName, getDebugName
  note    English wins over every other language wherever it stands (a Windows record in American English, then in another English, a Unicode record, a Macintosh Roman record, then any other Windows language), as fontTools' `getDebugName` does; a Macintosh Roman string with a byte past 127 reads that byte as the replacement character

capability font/metrics  the scale, the vertical metrics, the heights, the slant, the underline, the box, the weight and the style bits
  heroes  font.metrics_of, font.Metrics, font.outlines_of, font.Outlines
  go      Font.Metrics, Font.UnitsPerEm, Font.NumGlyphs, Font.Bounds, Font.PostTable, sfnt.PostTable, sfnt.Units, font.Metrics, font.Weight, font.Style, font.Stretch
  rust    Face::units_per_em, number_of_glyphs, ascender, descender, line_gap, capital_height, x_height, italic_angle, underline_metrics, weight, width, style, is_bold, is_italic, is_oblique, is_monospaced, is_regular, is_variable, global_bounding_box, Weight, Width, Style, LineMetrics, Rect, head::Table, hhea::Table, maxp::Table, post::Table
  python  head, hhea, maxp, post and OS/2 table fields, getGlyphCount
  note    the ascent, descent and line gap are `hhea`'s, and `OS/2`'s typographic ones where `hhea` holds zeros, as CoreText and FreeType read them; Go's `Metrics` is those scaled to a size and rounded, which a program multiplies out

capability font/cmap  the glyph a character has, and every character a font has a glyph for
  heroes  font.glyph_of, font.code_points_of
  go      Font.GlyphIndex, sfnt.GlyphIndex
  rust    Face::glyph_index, Face::tables, Format, cmap::Subtables, cmap::Subtable, cmap::Subtable0, cmap::Subtable4, cmap::Subtable6, cmap::Subtable10, cmap::Subtable12, cmap::Subtable13
  python  TTFont.getBestCmap, getGlyphID, getReverseGlyphMap
  note    the subtable chosen is the Unicode one the specification prefers: Windows full repertoire, Unicode platform, Windows BMP, Windows symbol; `.notdef` is never answered, where Go answers 0 for a missing character

capability font/cmap-legacy  the legacy byte encodings of a font (cmap format 2) and its variation selectors (format 14)
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    cmap::Subtable2, cmap::Subtable14, Face::glyph_variation_index
  python  table__c_m_a_p.cmap formats 2 and 14
  note    the specification's two formats that map no single Unicode scalar to a glyph by themselves

capability font/advance  the advance and the left side bearing of a glyph
  heroes  font.advance_of, font.bearing_of
  go      Font.GlyphAdvance
  rust    Face::glyph_hor_advance, Face::glyph_hor_side_bearing, hmtx::Table, hmtx::Metrics, Face::glyph_phantom_points, PhantomPoints
  python  TTFont['hmtx']
  note    in font units, never scaled or hinted, where Go's are in 26.6 fixed point at a size

capability font/bounds  the box a glyph's points lie in
  heroes  font.bounds_of, font.Box
  go      Font.GlyphBounds
  rust    Face::glyph_bounding_box, Rect, RectF
  python  fontTools.pens.boundsPen.ControlBoundsPen
  note    the control points count, as `glyf`'s own box does and fontTools' `ControlBoundsPen` does; Go's and ttf-parser's `BoundsPen`-like boxes measure the curve itself

capability font/outline  a glyph's outline as lines and curves, composites expanded
  heroes  font.outline_of, font.Outline, font.Segment, font.to_cubics
  go      Font.LoadGlyph, sfnt.Segment, sfnt.Segments, sfnt.SegmentOp, sfnt.LoadGlyphOptions
  rust    Face::outline_glyph, OutlineBuilder, glyf::Table, cff::Table, cff::Matrix, Transform, Fixed
  python  TTFont.getGlyphSet, fontTools.pens.basePen.BasePen, fontTools.pens.recordingPen.RecordingPen, fontTools.pens.t2CharStringPen, fontTools.pens.ttGlyphPen
  note    TrueType contours start where fontTools starts them (on the contour's first on-curve point, else the middle of its last and first points), a run of off-curve points is a chain of quadratic curves, a contour of one point has no step; CFF's hints are read and left, its four flex operators, subroutines (local and global, up to 10 deep) and arithmetic are run; the deprecated accented `endchar` is `unsupported`; unhinted, at font units, where Go scales to a size

capability font/text-width  the width of a text set in a font, and whether the font can show it
  heroes  font.width_of, font.has_glyphs
  go      font.MeasureString, font.MeasureBytes, font.BoundString, font.BoundBytes, font.Drawer.MeasureString, font.Drawer.MeasureBytes, font.Drawer.BoundString, font.Drawer.BoundBytes
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the sum of the advances, never kerned, as `pdf.width_of` does not kern the standard fonts; a character the font lacks fails `not_found` naming it, where Go measures `.notdef`

capability font/kern  the adjustment between two glyphs side by side from the `kern` table
  heroes  font.kern_of
  go      Font.Kern, font.Face.Kern
  rust    kern::Table, kern::Subtables, kern::Subtable, kern::Subtable0, kern::KerningPair
  python  TTFont['kern']
  note    format 0 horizontal subtables are read and their adjustments added; the formats 2 and 3 of the specification and Apple's `kerx` are not

capability font/tables  the tags of a font's tables and the bytes of each
  heroes  font.table_tags, font.table_of
  go      -
  rust    RawFace::table, RawFace::table_records, Face::table_data, Face::tables, FaceTables
  python  TTFont.keys, getTableData, TTFont.get
  note    a table is the file's bytes as they stand, no table of them decoded

capability font/subset  a TrueType font cut down to the glyphs a document uses, as a font file
  heroes  font.subset, font.subset_glyphs, font.subset_numbered, font.subset_glyph_of, font.Subset
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  fontTools.subset.Subsetter, fontTools.subset.Options, fontTools.subset.subset_glyphs, fontTools.subset.closure_glyphs, fontTools.subset.load_font, fontTools.subset.save_font, fontTools.subset.main
  note    glyphs are renumbered from 0 in the order of the original (`.notdef` first), the components of every composite come along and are renumbered, the glyphs keep their instructions with `cvt `, `fpgm` and `prep`, `loca` is written with 32-bit offsets and `post` as format 3 (no glyph names); the layout tables (`GPOS`, `GSUB`, `GDEF`) are left out, where fontTools keeps those of the features asked for; the tables of the result are read back by fontTools 4.68.0 and by Poppler's `pdftotext` through `pdf`; `font.subset_numbered` keeps every glyph at its own number instead, the others empty with no advance, so that a PDF's codes are final before the glyphs it uses are all known

capability font/subset-cff  a CFF font cut down to the glyphs a document uses
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  fontTools.subset (CFF outlines), fontTools.subset.cff, fontTools.subset.desubroutinize, fontTools.subset.remove_unused_subroutines
  note    needs the Top DICT, the charset and the subroutines rewritten; a CFF font is `unsupported` to `font.subset` meanwhile, never cut badly

